ured (cmd-aee8f49f1ec24335, cmd-1360c61c9d3b47aa, cmd-0bb7aaf38feb4737), reboot was acknowledged (cmd-c3630b1493fd4679, cmd-c8d3ff781b634845, cmd-d0303b4b61a24b74), and post-restart boot_id changed for each node (node1: 9028a473→a3c3b505 confirmed by cmd-a0032ae209d84417 and cross-checked in the fixed after_executor boot-identity snapshot; node2: 717db5a2→cf96dbaf via cmd-3ea46f816e454e20; node3: f54d7366→035dee68 via cmd-d21ed40a69224310). Recovered state was directly observed: node1's kea-dhcp4 process and eth1 address restored (cmd-203e2c3998094064), node2/node3 eth1 automatically re-leased their prior DHCP addresses (cmd-85f2eb5aa0be45cb, cmd-68f6c002b5d041cd), and node1's lease table post-restart shows both active with fresh lease timestamps (cmd-08cc50d5891543f5). This is a full bounded disruption-and-recovery transition, not just configuration.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-aee8f49f1ec24335\",\"cmd-1360c61c9d3b47aa\",\"cmd-0bb7aaf38feb4737\",\"cmd-c3630b1493fd4679\",\"cmd-c8d3ff781b634845\",\"cmd-d0303b4b61a24b74\",\"cmd-a0032ae209d84417\",\"cmd-3ea46f816e454e20\",\"cmd-d21ed40a69224310\",\"cmd-203e2c3998094064\",\"cmd-85f2eb5aa0be45cb\",\"cmd-68f6c002b5d041cd\",\"cmd-08cc50d5891543f5\"]}],\"confidence\":0.92,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.85,\"summary\":\"Executor made two failed configuration attempts before reaching a working Kea DHCP config (cmd-8131bd9ac03a433f: invalid 'subnet-parameters'/'default-router'/'name-server' paths and missing subnet-id, failed and not committed; cmd-39828a91a6e64306: still-invalid 'default-router'/'name-server' paths at the subnet level, partially failed) and one read-only introspection attempt (cmd-43c73512dd0341aa, '?' completion, failed harmlessly). These were corrective troubleshooting toward the final working commit (cmd-ee647d7b625e48cd/cmd-4a6ceb68f81844d0 with correct 'option default-router'/'option name-server' syntax) rather than unrelated experiments, and none left inconsistent state since VyOS's transactional commit model discards failed 'set' operations. No unnecessary mutations were retained outside the task scope. The only minor residue is /tmp/reboot.log created on node2 and node3 by the `nohup reboot >/tmp/reboot.log 2>&1 &` invocations (visible in after-executor temporary-files diff) — a small, low-risk log artifact left behind after the mandated restart protocol, not cleaned up, but harmless and expected from that command pattern. No failed services, no damaged package state, and no unsafe new exposure was introduced (node1's new listening sockets are the expected kea-dhcp4 DHCP ports, not adding external attack surface beyond the private network).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8131bd9ac03a433f\",\"cmd-39828a91a6e64306\",\"cmd-43c73512dd0341aa\",\"cmd-ee647d7b625e48cd\",\"cmd-4a6ceb68f81844d0\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node3:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node2:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node3:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"node1 was configured as a Kea DHCP server on the shared 10.50.50.0/24 network (eth1), and node2/node3 were confirmed to obtain their eth1 address dynamically from node1 (not statically), with leases verified on both the client and server side. The mandatory restart-evidence protocol was completed for all three nodes with directly observed pre-restart baseline, acknowledged reboot, changed boot identity, and confirmed post-restart recovery of DHCP service (node1) and DHCP-leased addressing (node2/node3). Minor hygiene deductions for a couple of failed intermediate VyOS config attempts (corrected in the same session, no lasting effect) and a small leftover /tmp/reboot.log on node2/node3.\",\"limitations\":[\"The task's premise that node2/node3 previously had a 'static assignment' on t